GNU lightning is a library to aid in making portable programs
that compile assembly code at run time.  For more information,
look at the info documentation.

For help building lightning, see README-hacking.


Maintained port dependency
==========================

This branch preserves the complete upstream GNU lightning history and starts
from the signed project release lineage at tag ``lightning-2.2.3`` (commit
``815f4221e25cad4b81a17169ba8557aed503f18c``).  ``upstream`` points to GNU
Savannah.  The pinned Gnulib source is fetched from the GitHub mirror because
Savannah's anonymous HTTPS endpoint is frequently overloaded; the gitlink is
still the upstream revision selected by GNU lightning 2.2.3.

The maintained build interface is Python and produces a self-contained static
development prefix under ``build/install/<host>``:

    uv run --frozen python tools/build_lightning.py --clean --cc clang

The installed contract is ``include/lightning.h``, the target-specific headers
under ``include/lightning/``, ``lib/liblightning.a``, and
``lib/pkgconfig/lightning.pc``.  Consumers may point CMake's
``find_path(lightning.h)`` and ``find_library(lightning)`` at that prefix; they
do not need a runner-specific ``liblightning-dev`` package.

Run the complete asset-free Linux contract with:

    uv run --frozen python tools/verify.py --cc clang

That gate runs GNU lightning's 135 upstream JIT tests, installs the library,
then compiles and executes a program against the installed prefix.  The
program emits and calls native code, so a passing build alone cannot
misrepresent an executable-memory failure as support.

The library remains GNU LGPL v3 or later; build and documentation files retain
their existing GNU license notices.  See ``COPYING.LESSER`` and the individual
source headers.  Downstream distribution must continue to satisfy those terms.

Built with AI assistance (Claude and other LLMs).
